
This is an FEP calculation for mutating cyclohexane to
tetrahydropyran in ethanol using the united-atom
force field. The solute and 260 solvent molecules 
are flexible. 

Examples of related FEP calcualtions can be found in testjob FEPaq;
this includes command files that do the window chaining internally
(there is only one cmd file for UNIX/Linux).

Short runs are made for four chained windows with
Equilibration = 0.2M; Averaging = 0.4M configurations.
Just submit 
csh -e f125cmd >& log125 &
to run all four windows.
Please also read the comments in the f125cmd file.

Time required: 1.1 hours on an R5000 SGI.

Results summary:

f125sum: DeltaG (  0.000 to   0.125)  =     -0.055 +/-     0.059
f125sum: DeltaG (  0.125 to   0.250)  =     -0.063 +/-     0.095
f375sum: DeltaG (  0.250 to   0.375)  =     -0.033 +/-     0.035
f375sum: DeltaG (  0.375 to   0.500)  =     -0.011 +/-     0.053
f625sum: DeltaG (  0.500 to   0.625)  =     -0.006 +/-     0.041
f625sum: DeltaG (  0.625 to   0.875)  =      0.204 +/-     0.079
f875sum: DeltaG (  0.750 to   0.875)  =     -0.139 +/-     0.045
f875sum: DeltaG (  0.875 to   1.000)  =     -0.148 +/-     0.069

Note:
1. The sum of deltaGs is NOT the difference in free energies of solvation.
The same fep would be needed in the gas phase. The difference in the gas and
solvent phase computed deltaGs would be the delta delta G of solvation.
The gas phase calculation is needed because the solute is flexible and
intramolecular energy terms are contributing to the deltaGs.

2. The runs need to be longer, e.g., 1M equilibration and 2-4M averaging. 
